'''''In the Mood for Love''''' ({{Lang-zh|t=花樣年華|first=t|l=Flower-like Years, the prime of one's youth|s=花样年华}}) is a 2000 [[romantic drama]] film written, directed, and produced by [[Wong Kar-wai]]. An [[international co-production]] between [[Cinema of Hong Kong|Hong Kong]] and [[Cinema of France|France]], the film follows a man ([[Tony Leung Chiu-wai|Tony Leung]]) and a woman ([[Maggie Cheung]]) in 1962 who discover that their spouses are having an affair. As they spend time together, they gradually develop feelings for one another. It is the second installment in an informal [[trilogy]], preceded by ''[[Days of Being Wild]]'' (1990) and followed by [[2046 (film)|''2046'']] (2004). 

The film premiered in the official competition at the [[2000 Cannes Film Festival|53rd Cannes Film Festival]],<ref name="David Ng2">{{cite web |title=Images – In the Mood for Love |url=http://www.imagesjournal.com/issue10/reviews/inthemood2/ |access-date=13 March 2016 |work=imagesjournal.com}}</ref> where it received acclaim. Leung won the [[Cannes Film Festival Award for Best Actor|Best Actor]] award, becoming the first Hong Kong actor to receive the honour. ''In the Mood for Love'' was selected as [[List of Hong Kong submissions for the Academy Award for Best International Feature Film|Hong Kong's submission]] for [[Academy Award for Best International Feature Film|Best International Feature Film]] at the [[73rd Academy Awards]], though it was not nominated.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Whipp |first1=Glenn |last2=Chang |first2=Justin |date=2021-03-21 |title=Oscars 2001 rewind: What won ... and what should have won |url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/movies/story/2021-03-21/oscars-rewind-2001-critics-ballots |access-date=2024-09-15 |website=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}</ref> It is often listed as one of the [[List of films voted the best|greatest films of all time]] and one of the major works of [[Cinema of Asia|Asian cinema]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-05-18 |title=How In the Mood for Love became a modern masterpiece – 20 years on |url=https://www.scmp.com/magazines/style/article/3084877/how-wong-kar-wais-mood-love-became-modern-masterpiece-20-years |access-date=2022-04-19 |website=South China Morning Post}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=In the Mood for Love (2000) |url=https://www.bfi.org.uk/film/1b8c3bcb-842f-5ba2-90ee-39b6b40b7c21/in-the-mood-for-love |access-date=2022-12-01 |website=BFI}}</ref>

