'''''The Notebook''''' is a 2004 American [[Romance film#Romantic drama|romantic drama]] film directed by [[Nick Cassavetes]], from a screenplay by [[Jeremy Leven]] and [[Jan Sardi]], and based on [[The Notebook (novel)|the 1996 novel]] by [[Nicholas Sparks]]. The film stars [[Ryan Gosling]] and [[Rachel McAdams]] as a young couple who fall in love in the 1940s. Their story is read from a notebook in the modern day by an elderly man telling the tale to a fellow nursing home resident.

''The Notebook'' had its world premiere at the [[Seattle International Film Festival]] on May 20, 2004, and was theatrically released in the United States on June 25, 2004 by [[New Line Cinema]]. Despite generally mixed reviews from critics, Gosling and McAdams were singled out for praise for their performances. The film was a sleeper hit at the box office, grossing $118.3 million against its $29 million budget, and has become a [[Cult following|cult classic]] in the years since its release.<ref name="BoxOfficeMojo"/><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Fleming |first=Michael |date=March 19, 2007 |url=https://variety.com/2007/film/news/sparks-adaptation-is-dear-to-tatum-1117961437/ |title=Sparks Adaptation Is 'Dear' to Tatum |magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |access-date=October 10, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.seventeen.com/celebrity/movies-tv/a32718/the-notebook-facts/| title=4 Ways 'The Notebook' Rewrote the Weepie and Changed Hollywood| publisher=[[Yahoo! Movies]] | date=June 25, 2014| first=Oliver| last=Lyttelton| access-date=October 10, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine| url=https://time.com/2919207/best-notebook-quotes-anniversary/| title=9 Best Quotes from The Notebook| date=June 25, 2014| first=Joan E.| last=Greve| magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]| access-date=July 26, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.nme.com/filmandtv/news/the-notebook-director-claims-ryan-gosling-tried-to/342856| title='The Notebook' director claims Ryan Gosling tried to have Rachel McAdams removed from film. There is also an encore from Jake Gyllenhaal where he appears as the bus driver at 53:04.| date=July 3, 2014| work=[[NME]]| access-date=July 26, 2014}}</ref> On November 11, 2012, an extended version premiered on [[History of Freeform#ABC Family|ABC Family]] with deleted scenes added back into the original storyline.<ref>{{cite web| url=https://nicholassparks.com/news/2012/the-notebook-special-edition-premieres-on-abc-family-this-sunday/| title=The Notebook Special Edition on ABC Family This Sunday| publisher=Nicholas Sparks| date=November 8, 2012| access-date=2022-10-22}}</ref>

The film earned several accolades, including the [[MTV Movie Award for Best Kiss]] for Gosling and McAdams at the [[2005 MTV Movie Awards]]. At the [[11th Screen Actors Guild Awards]], [[James Garner]] was nominated for [[Screen Actors Guild Award for Outstanding Performance by a Male Actor in a Supporting Role|Outstanding Performance by a Male Actor in a Supporting Role]] and the director's mother, [[Gena Rowlands]], won [[Satellite Award for Best Supporting Actress – Motion Picture|Best Supporting Actress – Drama]] at the [[9th Golden Satellite Awards]].

