'''''Crazy Rich Asians''''' is a 2018 American romantic [[comedy drama]] film directed by [[Jon M. Chu]] from a screenplay by Peter Chiarelli and [[Adele Lim]], based on the [[Crazy Rich Asians (novel)|2013 novel]] by [[Kevin Kwan]]. The film stars [[Constance Wu]], [[Henry Golding]], [[Gemma Chan]], [[Lisa Lu]], [[Awkwafina]], [[Ken Jeong]], and [[Michelle Yeoh]]. It follows a [[Chinese Americans|Chinese American]] professor, Rachel, who travels to [[Singapore]] with her boyfriend Nick and is shocked to discover that Nick's family is one of the richest families in Singapore.

The film was announced in August 2012 after the rights to the book were purchased. Many of the cast members signed on in the spring of 2017, and filming took place from April to June of that year in parts of Singapore, Malaysia, and New York City. It is the first film by a [[major film studio|major Hollywood studio]] to feature a majority cast of Chinese descent in a modern setting since ''[[The Joy Luck Club (film)|The Joy Luck Club]]'' in 1993.<!--(Memoirs of a Geisha was a period piece)--> Despite praise in the United States for its Asian representation, the film also received criticism for casting few actors from Singapore, from Southeast Asia, or of [[Overseas Chinese|fully ethnically Chinese]] heritage; for not fully portraying Singapore's [[Demographics of Singapore|multiracial population]], and, some said, for perpetuating [[Stereotypes of East Asians in the United States|stereotypes of East Asians]].

''Crazy Rich Asians'' premiered on August 7, 2018, at the [[TCL Chinese Theatre]] in [[Los Angeles]] and was released theatrically in the United States on August 15, 2018, by [[Warner Bros. Pictures]]. The film grossed over $238 million against a $30 million budget, making it the highest-grossing romantic comedy of the 2010s, and received praise for the performances of the cast, screenplay, and production design. It received nominations for two [[76th Golden Globe Awards|Golden Globe Awards]] (including [[Golden Globe Award for Best Motion Picture – Musical or Comedy|Best Motion Picture – Musical or Comedy]]), three [[NAACP Image Awards]] (including [[NAACP Image Award for Outstanding Motion Picture|Outstanding Motion Picture]]), four [[24th Critics' Choice Awards|Critics' Choice Awards]] (winning [[Critics' Choice Movie Award for Best Comedy|Best Comedy]]), and a [[Screen Actors Guild Award for Outstanding Performance by a Cast in a Motion Picture]]. {{As of|2025|November}}, two sequels, based on the novel's follow-ups ''[[China Rich Girlfriend]]'' and ''[[Rich People Problems]]'', have been in and out of development for over seven years.

