'''''Crash Landing on You''''' ({{Korean|사랑의 불시착}}) is a 2019 South Korean [[romantic comedy]] television series written by [[Park Ji-eun]], directed by [[Lee Jung-hyo (director)|Lee Jung-hyo]], and starring [[Hyun Bin]], [[Son Ye-jin]], [[Kim Jung-hyun (actor, born 1990)|Kim Jung-hyun]], and [[Seo Ji-hye]]. The series follows Yoon Se-ri, a South Korean chaebol heiress who is swept across the border by a tornado while [[paragliding]] and lands in North Korea. She meets Ri Jeong-hyeok, a captain in the [[Korean People's Army]], who hides and protects her. Despite [[North Korea–South Korea relations|the political division]] [[Korea|between their countries]], they develop a romantic relationship. A subplot involves Gu Seung-jun, a fraudster seeking refuge in the North, and Seo Dan, a musician and Jeong-hyeok's fiancée.

Park Ji-eun conceived ''Crash Landing on You'' based on an incident in which a South Korean actress drifted into North Korean waters. The script draws thematic inspiration from the paradox of the [[Division of Korea|Korean division]], as well as literary works such as ''[[The Wonderful Wizard of Oz]]'' and ''[[The Little Prince]]''. Produced by [[Studio Dragon]] and [[Culture Depot]] with a budget of approximately US$20&nbsp;million, the series was filmed over six months. The production used live-shooting, and filming took place in Switzerland, Mongolia, and South Korea. [[Nam Hye-seung]] composed and produced the original soundtrack.

Comprising 16 episodes, ''Crash Landing on You'' aired on [[tvN (South Korean TV channel)|tvN]] from December 14, 2019, to February 16, 2020, and was distributed globally by [[Netflix]]. The series received critical acclaim for its cinematography, performances, and the chemistry between the lead actors, as well as its portrayal of life in North Korea. It faced domestic criticism; the [[Christian Liberty Unification Party]] accused the show of violating the [[National Security Act (South Korea)|National Security Act]] by "glamorizing" North Korea, while others criticized its use of [[product placement]]. [[Mass media in North Korea|North Korean state media]] condemned the series as a "provocative" distortion. Commercially, it was a major success, achieving a nationwide rating of 21.7% for its finale and becoming [[Korean drama#List of highest-rated series on cable television|the highest-rated drama in tvN's history]] at the time.

Portraying North Korea's daily life against the backdrop of the peninsula's ideological division, ''Crash Landing on You'' was a major success of the [[Korean Wave]], particularly in Japan; it drove sales for featured brands and increased tourism to its filming locations in Switzerland. The series also circulated in North Korea via [[Jangmadang|the black market]], where a line of dialogue from the show became a trend. The series was adapted into a musical and received numerous accolades, including the Grand Prize for Hyun Bin at the [[7th APAN Star Awards]] and multiple nominations at the [[56th Baeksang Arts Awards]]. For her contributions to cultural understanding through the series, the [[Ministry of Unification]] named Park Ji-eun the "Unification Education Person of the Year".

selected_sections.raw_wikitext:
''Crash Landing on You'' depicts national division and was broadcast against a backdrop of [[Panmunjom Declaration|improving relations between the two Koreas]].{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|p=506}} Rather than focusing on [[Korean conflict|conflict]] or [[Korean reunification|reunification]], the screenplay explores the possibility of coexistence, presenting the nuances between the nations.{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|pp=503, 506}} The series portrays the intersection of a chaebol heiress and a soldier; their respective personifications of [[capitalism]] and [[socialism]] render the work ideologically inseparable.<ref name="Min Kyung-won 2019.12.19"/> Journalist and education critic Shin Nam-ho and scholar Doyle Greene noted that the ideological conflict between North and South is the obstacle to the protagonists' romance.<ref name="Shin Nam-ho 2020.02.17">{{Cite news |last=Shin |first=Nam-ho |date=February 17, 2020 |script-title=ko:[신남호 교육칼럼] 드라마 '사랑의 불시착'과 통일 교육 |trans-title=[Shin Nam-ho's Education Column] The drama 'Crash Landing on You' and unification education |url=https://www.vop.co.kr/A00001468984.html |newspaper=[[Voice of the People (South Korea)|Voice of the People]] |language=ko |access-date=November 16, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251116133257/https://www.vop.co.kr/A00001468984.html |archive-date=November 16, 2025 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Doyle Greene">{{Cite journal |first=Doyle |last=Greene |year=2023 |title=A United Korea (Or, the Great Divide): Crash Landing on You |journal=Film Criticism |language=en |volume=47 |issue=1 |issn=2471-4364 |doi=10.3998/fc.5175 |doi-access=free}}</ref> Scholars Stephen Epstein and Christopher K. Green regard ''Crash Landing on You'' as a significant text, calling it the "most noteworthy South Korean [...] representation of North Korea".{{sfn|Epstein|Green|2020|p=1}} Scholar Indumathi Somashekar and cultural critic Kim Heon-sik stated that the drama changed perceptions by demonstrating the humanity and social values of North Koreans.{{sfn|Somashekar|2023|pp=479–480}}<ref name="Kim Heon-sik">{{Cite news |last=Kim |first=Heon-sik |editor-last1=Lee Jin-seo |date=June 6, 2023 |script-title=ko:[드라마, 알고 봐야 재밌다] 사랑의 불시착, 드라마가 맺어준 인연 |trans-title=[Drama, Fun to Watch if You Know] Crash Landing on You, a connection made by drama |url=https://www.rfa.org/korean/weekly_program/bb38d654/b4dcb77cb9c8-c54cace0-bd10c57c-c7acbc0cb2e4/kdrama-06052023145925.html |publisher=[[Radio Free Asia|Radio Free Asia Korean]] |language=ko |access-date=November 16, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230607025334/https://www.rfa.org/korean/weekly_program/bb38d654/b4dcb77cb9c8-c54cace0-bd10c57c-c7acbc0cb2e4/kdrama-06052023145925.html |archive-date=June 7, 2023 |url-status=live |ref=none}}</ref>

Son and Schwak argue that ''Crash Landing on You'' portrays North Korea with nostalgia, as "the receptacle of a Korean past characterized by ruralness and intimate community",{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|p=504}} or a version of South Korea's "pre-development".{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|pp=508–509}} This imagery is conveyed through the contrast between the "visually muted North" and the "technicolor South",{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|p=511}} and the North Korean characters' astonishment at the modernity and affluence of Seoul.{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|p=512}} The series depicts North Korea as a [[Totalitarianism|totalitarian]] regime marked by [[Human rights in North Korea|oppression]] and [[Corruption in North Korea|corruption]],{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|p=510}} whereas South Korea is presented as a compassionate state, evidenced by the [[National Intelligence Service (South Korea)|National Intelligence Service]]'s (NIS) humane treatment of the protagonists.<ref name="Shin Nam-ho 2020.02.17"/> The work highlights the darker side of South Korean capitalism through Se-ri's greedy brothers.{{sfn|Somashekar|2023|p=482}} Shin, Son, and Schwak each observe that the protagonists' reunion in Switzerland reflects a forced compromise and the "untimeliness of reunification"; Shin remarks that their love cannot be realized on the Korean peninsula.<ref name="Shin Nam-ho 2020.02.17"/>{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|p=514}}

Critic Hwang Jin-mi described the use of the North Korean setting in ''Crash Landing on You'' as a "masterstroke" for the [[romantic comedy]] genre. This setting generates the "forbidden" element, evoking [[William Shakespeare]]'s ''[[Romeo and Juliet]]'', and making geographical space the primary obstacle, obviating the need for a fictional country as in ''[[Descendants of the Sun]]'' (2016).<ref name="Hwang Jin-mi 2020.01.10">{{Cite news |last=Hwang |first=Jin-mi |date=January 10, 2020 |script-title=ko:'콘텐츠 블루오션' 북한의 재발견...드라마 '사랑의 불시착' |trans-title='Content Blue Ocean': Rediscovery of North Korea... Drama 'Crash Landing on You' |url=http://www.hani.co.kr/arti/culture/culture_general/923995.html |newspaper=[[Hankyoreh]] |language=ko |access-date=November 16, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200110122733/http://www.hani.co.kr/arti/culture/culture_general/923995.html |archive-date=January 10, 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref> The series employs K-drama tropes such as "meant-to-be" encounters and "accidental skinship",{{sfn|An|2022|p=133}} yet updates or reverses gender roles,{{efn|One example of reversed gender roles is the portrayal of the female protagonist, Yoon Se-ri, as a chaebol, a character archetype typically reserved for male leads.{{sfn|An|2022|p=135}}}}{{sfnm|1a1=An|1y=2022|1p=135|2a1=Shin|2y=2022|2p=54}} and mocks South Korean filmmaking stereotypes.{{efn|The series satirizes dramatic tropes through the character Kim Ju-meok, who, upon watching South Korean dramas, argues that [[amnesia]] is a "common illness in capitalistic countries".{{sfn|An|2022|p=140}}}}{{sfn|An|2022|p=140}} The "male as saviour" and "female as damsel" tropes are reworked, as the protagonists risk their lives to save each other.{{sfn|An|2022|p=136}} Scholar An Ji-yoon describes the aesthetic of ''Crash Landing on You'' as quirky and fairy tale-like.{{sfn|An|2022|pp=143–144}} According to scholar Kim Jeong-hee, North Korean dialects with explanatory subtitles and pop culture references, such as the boy band BTS, attracted viewers.{{sfn|Kim|2023|pp=324–325}}

''Crash Landing on You'' was highly acclaimed for its re-creation of life in North Korea, based on research and consultation with defectors. Journalist Joo Sung-ha, a defector, remarked that the series accurately depicted many aspects, from citizens secretly accessing South Korean media to morning [[Propaganda in North Korea|propaganda]] exercises.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Park |first=Eun-hae |date=December 16, 2019 |script-title=ko:'사랑의 불시착' 탈북자 출신 기자도 극찬한 북한 고증은? |trans-title='Crash Landing on You': What is the historical accuracy regarding North Korea praised by a defector journalist? |url=https://www.kookje.co.kr/news2011/asp/newsbody.asp?code=0500&key=20191216.99099007305 |newspaper=[[The Kookje Daily News]] |language=ko |access-date=November 16, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251116133324/https://www.kookje.co.kr/news2011/asp/newsbody.asp?code=0500&key=20191216.99099007305 |archive-date=November 16, 2025 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="BTS 2019.12.21">{{Cite news |last=Choi |first=Ji-soo |date=December 21, 2019 |script-title=ko:'사랑의 불시착' 제작 비하인드 ② |trans-title='Crash Landing on You' Behind the Scenes 2 |url=https://www.kookje.co.kr/news2011/asp/newsbody.asp?code=0500&key=20191221.99099010087 |newspaper=[[The Kookje Daily News]] |language=ko |access-date=November 16, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251116133409/https://www.kookje.co.kr/news2011/asp/newsbody.asp?code=0500&key=20191221.99099010087 |archive-date=November 16, 2025 |url-status=live }}</ref> The series highlights resource scarcity through frequent power outages, bicycle-powered televisions, and the stockpiling of batteries.<ref name="Min Kyung-won 2019.12.19"/><ref name="BTS 2019.12.21"/> Other depictions of daily hardship include bathing in washbasins during winter and using seawater to make kimchi due to salt shortages.<ref name="BTS 2019.12.21"/> The series depicts ''jangmadang'' as sites for the illicit trade of South Korean products,<ref name="BTS 2019.12.21"/>{{sfn|Son|Schwak|2022|p=515}} and illustrates the life of the Pyongyang elite as similar to that in a capitalist society.<ref name="Hwang Jin-mi 2020.01.10"/> The drama does not shy away from realities such as unannounced [[home inspection]]s, [[Mass surveillance in North Korea|mass surveillance]], and corruption.{{sfn|Somashekar|2023|p=481}}<ref name="Hwang Jin-mi 2020.01.10"/><ref name="BTS 2019.12.21"/>

